<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

  <!--
    Room fingerprinting. Android gates Wi-Fi scan results behind location
    permission because the list of nearby access points can be used to locate a
    device; Aware never stores the network names or a position, only a hash of
    the set (07-DATA-PRIVACY: "Wi-Fi/Bluetooth hashes over device names").

    NEARBY_WIFI_DEVICES is the Android 13+ replacement, declared alongside the
    legacy permission with maxSdkVersion so older devices keep working.
  -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_WIFI_STATE" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION"
                   android:maxSdkVersion="32" />
  <!--
    KNOWN BROKEN, measured on a Pixel 8 (2026-08-07): the Wi-Fi signal never
    reaches the fingerprint on Android 13+. NEARBY_WIFI_DEVICES is granted, yet
    WifiManager.getScanResults() is refused:

      W WifiService: Permission violation - getScanResults not allowed for
      uid=..., packageName=com.companyname.aware,
      reason=java.lang.SecurityException: UID ... has no location permission

    ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION above is capped at SDK 32, so the app holds no location
    permission and the call returns an empty list. Adding
    android:usesPermissionFlags="neverForLocation" here does NOT lift the gate —
    that was tried and measured, and the same SecurityException came back.

    The failure is silent: no prompt, no exception in managed code, just an empty
    result. So the sharpest discriminator in the match (weight 0.45) contributes
    nothing, and matching runs on three signals renormalized from 0.55, where
    pressure is identical on one floor and the magnetic direction is dominated by
    the Earth's field. The floor score between two rooms in one home is then
    ~0.77 — above the 0.6 bar — so every room recognizes as every other room.

    Fixing it means choosing: hold ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION at runtime (works, but
    asks for location in an app whose promise is that nothing leaves the device),
    or drop Wi-Fi and make the remaining signals discriminate. Not decided.
  -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.NEARBY_WIFI_DEVICES" />

  <!--
    Bluetooth is the signal that replaces Wi-Fi. neverForLocation genuinely works
    here — unlike getScanResults above, a BLE scan under BLUETOOTH_SCAN with this
    flag needs no location permission, which is what keeps Aware location-free.

    Only advertised device *names* are hashed, never addresses: BLE addresses are
    resolvable-private on most modern hardware and rotate every few minutes, which
    would change a room's identity while the phone sat still.
  -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH_SCAN"
                   android:usesPermissionFlags="neverForLocation" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH" android:maxSdkVersion="30" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.BLUETOOTH_ADMIN" android:maxSdkVersion="30" />

  <!-- The magnetometer, light and pressure sensors need no permission, and none
       of them is required: a device without one simply contributes fewer
       signals to the match. -->
  <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.sensor.compass" android:required="false" />
  <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.sensor.light" android:required="false" />
  <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.sensor.barometer" android:required="false" />

  <application android:allowBackup="true" android:supportsRtl="true"></application>
</manifest>
